      "body": "## Vulnerability details\n\nHyperliquid's 0x811 precompile (PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE) returns the full BorrowLendUserTokenState struct containing four uint64 fields: borrow.basis, borrow.value, supply.basis, supply.value. PrecompileReader.suppliedBalance() only extracts the fourth field (supply.value) when decoding, completely discarding the borrow information in the first two fields.\n\n```solidity\nfunction suppliedBalance(address account, uint64 token) internal view returns (uint64 supplied) {\n    (bool ok, bytes memory res) = HyperCoreConstants.PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE.staticcall(\n        abi.encode(account, token)\n    );\n    require(ok && res.length >= 128, \"PrecompileReader: supplied balance read failed\");\n    (,,, supplied) = abi.decode(res, (uint64, uint64, uint64, uint64));\n}\n```\n\nMonetrixAccountant._readL1Backing() iterates over all registered SuppliedAssets, accumulating supply values into total via suppliedUsdcEvm() or suppliedNotionalUsdcFromPerp(), without ever subtracting borrow values at any point.\n\n```solidity\nuint256 slen = suppliedList.length;\nfor (uint256 i = 0; i < slen; i++) {\n    SuppliedAsset storage a = suppliedList[i];\n    if (a.spotToken == uint64(HyperCoreConstants.USDC_TOKEN_INDEX)) {\n        total += int256(PrecompileReader.suppliedUsdcEvm(account));\n    } else {\n        total += int256(\n            PrecompileReader.suppliedNotionalUsdcFromPerp(uint32(a.spotToken), a.perpIndex, account)\n        );\n    }\n}\n```\n\nPM is a fully planned and implemented feature path in the protocol, not a hypothetical scenario. The contracts contain setPmEnabled(), notifyVaultSupply(), PM branches in _sendL1Bridge(), and the entire 0x811 read pipeline, all specifically written for PM mode. Governor enabling PM via setPmEnabled(true) is an expected governance operation. Once PM is enabled, the Vault automatically registers 0x811 supplied slots in the Accountant when executing hedges and BLP operations, through executeHedge() and supplyToBlp().\n\nAfter PM is enabled, auto-borrow requires no attacker to trigger. The Hyperliquid PM engine automatically manages the Vault's L1 account borrowing based on market conditions (funding rate changes, collateral value fluctuations, margin requirement adjustments). This is a core PM mechanism, not an edge case. The Vault contract only has sendSupply (op=0) and sendWithdrawSupply (op=1) operations, with no explicit borrow operation, suggesting the protocol developers may not have anticipated that PM would produce borrow liabilities, which explains why PrecompileReader completely ignores the borrow fields.\n\ntotalBackingSigned() is therefore permanently inflated: suppose the Vault supplies 500,000 USDC and 200,000 USDC is auto-borrowed, the net value should be 300,000 USDC, but the Accountant counts the full 500,000 USDC. surplus() and distributableSurplus() inherit this inflated value. When the Operator calls settle(proposedYield) as part of normal duties, Gate 3 checks proposedYield <= distributableSurplus(). Because distributableSurplus includes 200,000 USDC of phantom surplus, Gate 3 does not block this yield distribution that should not have passed. settle() then transfers real EVM USDC into YieldEscrow, and the Operator distributes it via distributeYield() to sUSDM holders, InsuranceFund, and Foundation. This constitutes direct loss of real funds, not merely functional impairment or availability issues.\n\nThis loss automatically repeats every settlement cycle without any human intervention. Gate 4's annualized APR cap (default 1200bps = 12%) only limits the extraction rate per settlement, it does not prevent cumulative losses. With 10M USDM supply, each 21-hour settlement cycle extracts approximately 6,904 USDC of phantom yield. Over 30 days across approximately 34 settlement cycles, this accumulates to roughly 234,000 USDC of real funds extracted. If the borrow persists or grows, the protocol will trend toward insolvency (backing < totalSupply) within months, violating the INV-1 solvency invariant. There is no on-chain alerting mechanism that can detect the inflated surplus, because totalBackingSigned() itself is the on-chain \"source of truth.\" Additionally, yieldShortfall() also uses the inflated surplus to determine how much yield to bridge from L1, further amplifying the fund outflow.\n\nThe attack path of this vulnerability does not depend on any hypothetical external conditions. PM enablement is a planned governance operation on the protocol roadmap, auto-borrow is an inherent PM mechanism, and the Operator calls settle() as part of normal duties. No step in the entire chain requires malicious behavior or unreasonable assumptions. Per the C4 High definition \"Assets can be stolen/lost/compromised directly (or indirectly if there is a valid attack path that does not have hand-wavy hypotheticals)\", the real USDC extracted from the protocol each settlement cycle constitutes direct fund loss, and no hand-wavy hypotheticals exist in the attack path.",
